# Theming

Themes map semantic color roles to palette values. Light and dark are modes of a theme; the accent is selected separately.

## How it works

Import the global styles once, followed by the optional theme presets:

```css
@import '@aesir/css/aesir-global.css';
@import '@aesir/css/aesir-themes.css';
```

## Theme presets

Use `mist` for a lower-contrast theme:

```html
<html data-aesir-theme="mist">
  ...
</html>
```

The same preset supports both modes:

```html
<html class="dark" data-aesir-theme="mist">
  ...
</html>
```

The `protan-deutan` preset remaps status hues for protanopia and deuteranopia without changing the selected accent:

```html
<html data-aesir-theme="protan-deutan">
  ...
</html>
```

Use `tritan` for tritanopia. Both color-vision presets improve hue separation, but status must still include text, an icon, or another non-color cue.

<div class="color-vision-statuses" aria-label="Current status color mapping">
  <span class="color-vision-status color-vision-status--success">Success</span>
  <span class="color-vision-status color-vision-status--info">Info</span>
  <span class="color-vision-status color-vision-status--warning">Warning</span>
  <span class="color-vision-status color-vision-status--danger">Error</span>
</div>

Changing the accent does not reset the theme. Color-vision presets change status colors, not the accent.

Remove `data-aesir-theme` to return to the default mapping.

## Accent color

Blue is the default accent. Choose another built-in palette with `data-aesir-accent`:

```html
<html data-aesir-accent="purple">
  ...
</html>
```

Available values are `blue`, `purple`, `pink`, `orange`, `yellow`, `lime`, `green`, `teal`, and `cyan`. Red is reserved for destructive actions and error states.

The attribute can also be scoped to part of an interface:

```html
<section data-aesir-accent="teal">
  ...
</section>
```

Status colors stay independent of the accent.

For a custom palette, override the semantic variables after importing Aesir:

```css
:root {
  --ds-accent: oklch(0.61 0.24 310);
  --ds-accent-hover: oklch(0.56 0.24 310);
  --ds-accent-foreground: white;
  --ds-accent-surface: oklch(0.97 0.02 310);
  --ds-accent-surface-hover: oklch(0.94 0.05 310);
  --ds-accent-surface-active: oklch(0.91 0.08 310);
  --ds-accent-text: oklch(0.45 0.19 310);
  --ds-accent-text-muted: oklch(0.56 0.23 310);
  --ds-accent-border: oklch(0.86 0.1 310);
  --ds-accent-border-hover: oklch(0.8 0.13 310);
  --ds-accent-ring: oklch(0.61 0.24 310);
}
```

## Minimal setup

```html
<html class="dark">
  ...
</html>
```

Remove `dark` to use light mode.

Set the native browser color scheme alongside the class so scrollbars and form controls match:

```css
html {
  color-scheme: light;
}

html.dark {
  color-scheme: dark;
}
```

## Initialize before rendering

Apply the saved or system theme before the page renders to avoid a light-theme flash. Place this inline script in the document head before loading the application stylesheet:

```html
<script>
  const savedTheme = localStorage.getItem('theme');
  const prefersDark = window.matchMedia('(prefers-color-scheme: dark)').matches;
  const isDark = savedTheme ? savedTheme === 'dark' : prefersDark;

  document.documentElement.classList.toggle('dark', isDark);
</script>
```

## Generic mode toggle

```js
const root = document.documentElement;
const isDark = root.classList.toggle('dark');
localStorage.setItem('theme', isDark ? 'dark' : 'light');
```

```js
const saved = localStorage.getItem('theme');
if (saved === 'dark') document.documentElement.classList.add('dark');
```

## Svelte example

This example uses `mode-watcher`.

Install the optional theme helper:

```sh
npm install mode-watcher
```

```svelte
<script>
  import { mode, toggleMode, ModeWatcher } from 'mode-watcher';
  import { AesirButton } from '$lib/aesir/src/index.js';
</script>

<ModeWatcher />
<AesirButton variant="outline" onclick={toggleMode}>
  Current: {mode.current ?? 'system'}
</AesirButton>
```

`ModeWatcher` syncs the saved preference; `toggleMode` switches modes.

## Scoped theming

You can also scope dark theme to a subtree:

```html
<section class="dark">
  <!-- this section uses dark tokens -->
</section>
```

<style>
	.color-vision-statuses {
		display: flex;
		flex-wrap: wrap;
		gap: var(--ds-spacing-2);
		margin-block: var(--ds-spacing-4);
	}

	.color-vision-status {
		padding: var(--ds-spacing-1) var(--ds-spacing-2);
		border: var(--ds-spacing-px) solid;
		border-radius: var(--ds-radius-sm);
		font-size: var(--ds-font-size-sm);
		font-weight: var(--ds-font-weight-medium);
	}

	.color-vision-status--success {
		border-color: var(--ds-border-green);
		background: var(--ds-surface-green);
		color: var(--ds-foreground-green);
	}

	.color-vision-status--info {
		border-color: var(--ds-border-blue);
		background: var(--ds-surface-blue);
		color: var(--ds-foreground-blue);
	}

	.color-vision-status--warning {
		border-color: var(--ds-border-orange);
		background: var(--ds-surface-orange);
		color: var(--ds-foreground-orange);
	}

	.color-vision-status--danger {
		border-color: var(--ds-border-red);
		background: var(--ds-surface-red);
		color: var(--ds-foreground-red);
	}
</style>
